  <!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout warn">A GENUINELY SPECIALIZED, TECHNICAL AREA: this poster presents the confirmed structure and distinctive concepts of the credit institution accounting framework, without reproducing the full technical Formblatt detail governed by the separate Kreditinstituts-Rechnungslegungsverordnung (RechKredV) — consistent with how this series has handled other extensive, highly-technical cross-reference material.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Where the general corporate framework meets a genuinely different balance sheet reality</div>
      <div class="principle">Everything this series has documented about Bilanz/GuV structure, measurement, and disclosure assumes an ordinary trading or manufacturing enterprise. A bank's own balance sheet — dominated by financial instruments, deposits, interbank lending, and trading positions — simply doesn't fit that mould. §§340–340o exist precisely because §330's own Formblatt-delegation power (established many posters ago) recognised this: specialised industries need genuinely tailored presentation, not a forced application of the general format.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§340–340O MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Klarer Einblick, for a fundamentally different balance sheet</strong> — the SAME transparency objective this whole series has documented, achieved through industry-tailored structure rather than the general Kapitalgesellschaft format</span>
        <span><strong>Prudence, sector-intensified</strong> — the Fonds für allgemeine Bankrisiken (Section 8) reflects a MANDATORY, quantified prudential reserve well beyond anything the general Vorsichtsprinzip requires elsewhere in this series</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§340–340o cover, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core content</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>340c</span><span>GuV/Anhang — the trading book netting mechanism</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>340d</span><span>Maturity-banded disclosure (Anhang only)</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>340e</span><span>Measurement, echoing §253's dual approach</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>340f–340g</span><span>The distinctive general-banking-risk prudential reserve</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>340i, 340k, 340l</span><span>Konzernabschluss adaptations, audit, and disclosure</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Four Distinct Institution Types</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Genuinely broader than "banks" alone</div>
      <div class="principle">§340(1), (4), (4a), and (5) confirm this Subsection applies to FOUR distinct institution types: Kreditinstitute (credit institutions, per the Kreditwesengesetz's own definition), Finanzdienstleistungsinstitute (financial services institutions), Wertpapierinstitute (investment firms), and, per the Subsection's own current title, Zahlungsinstitute and E-Geld-Institute (payment institutions and e-money institutions) — a genuinely broader population than "banks" in the everyday sense, reflecting the modern financial-services landscape's own increasing diversity beyond traditional deposit-taking banks.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§340c — the Handelsbestand Netting Mechanism</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A single net figure for trading activity, rather than gross income and expense lines</div>
      <div class="principle">§340c(1) requires income or expense from the Handelsbestand (trading book) to be shown as a SINGLE NET DIFFERENCE figure — combining all income and expenses from financial-instrument and precious-metal trading transactions, together with related write-up income and write-down expense, and provisions for threatened losses on such transactions. §340c(2) permits a SIMILAR netting of write-downs and write-ups on Beteiligungen, shares in affiliated undertakings, and securities treated as fixed assets. This netting approach is a genuinely distinctive departure from the general prohibition on offsetting income/expense line items this series established for ordinary corporations.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Worked Example — Netting Trading Book Income and Expense</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">One net line, rather than a lengthy grossed-up presentation</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A bank's trading activity for the year</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A bank's trading book generates €12,400,000 of gains on financial instrument trading, €9,100,000 of losses on other trading positions, €800,000 of write-up income, and €600,000 of write-down expense, plus a €200,000 provision for a threatened trading loss.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Under §340c(1), the GuV shows a SINGLE net trading-book result: €12,400,000 − €9,100,000 + €800,000 − €600,000 − €200,000 = <strong>€3,300,000</strong> — one net figure, rather than five separate gross line items an ordinary corporate GuV structure would otherwise require under the general offsetting prohibition established much earlier in this series.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§340d — Fristengliederung (Maturity Banding), Anhang-Only</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A structured maturity breakdown, confined to the notes rather than the face of the Bilanz</div>
      <div class="principle">§340d requires receivables and liabilities to be broken down by REMAINING TERM (Restlaufzeit) — a structured maturity-banding disclosure introduced by the EU's own Bank Accounts Directive, following the same broader pattern as the general Bilanzrichtlinie and Konzernbilanzrichtlinie. Critically, these figures are disclosed EXCLUSIVELY in the Anhang, not on the face of the Bilanz itself — giving readers visibility into a bank's own asset/liability maturity profile (a genuinely central concern for assessing liquidity risk) without cluttering the primary balance sheet presentation.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — Maturity Band Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Giving readers a genuine liquidity-risk picture, in the notes</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Disclosing a bank's own loan maturity profile</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A bank's loan portfolio includes €40,000,000 maturing within 3 months, €65,000,000 maturing between 3 months and 1 year, and €120,000,000 maturing beyond 1 year.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The Bilanz itself shows only the AGGREGATE €225,000,000 receivables figure — the FULL maturity breakdown appears exclusively in the Anhang under §340d, giving readers genuine insight into the bank's own liquidity structure without expanding the primary balance sheet presentation itself.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§340e — Dual Measurement, Echoing §253 With Banking-Specific Detail</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The same Anlage/Umlaufvermögen dual approach this series established early on, applied to banking assets</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§340e(1) requires credit institutions to measure Beteiligungen (including shares in affiliated undertakings), business licences, industrial property rights, and similar assets according to their DEDICATION (Zweckbestimmung) — following either the fixed-asset measurement principles of §253(3), established in the very early Measurement poster, OR the current-asset principles of §253(4), depending on whether the asset is genuinely intended for permanent use in the business. This is conceptually the SAME dual-classification logic §247's own Anlage/Umlaufvermögen distinction established at the very start of this series, now applied with banking-specific detail to the kinds of assets a credit institution's balance sheet actually holds.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§340f–340g — the Distinctive Prudential Reserve for General Banking Risks</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely bank-specific, quantified prudential mechanism with no ordinary-corporate equivalent</div>
      <div class="principle">§340f permits credit institutions to make PRECAUTIONARY value adjustments (Vorsorge) on certain receivables and securities beyond what strict measurement rules alone would require, reflecting the PARTICULAR risks inherent in banking business — a genuinely distinctive, banking-specific prudence mechanism. §340g then requires a special reserve — the "Fonds für allgemeine Bankrisiken" (fund for general banking risks) — to be allocated EACH YEAR an amount of AT LEAST 10% of the net income from the Handelsbestand (the SAME trading-book result established in §340c above), shown as its OWN separate Bilanz item. This reserve may only be released under specific conditions, and any release must be disclosed and explained in the Anhang.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — the Mandatory 10% Trading-Income Allocation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A quantified, mandatory reserve — not merely a discretionary buffer</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: Calculating the mandatory Fonds für allgemeine Bankrisiken allocation</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Continuing Example 1: the bank's net Handelsbestand result for the year was €3,300,000.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Under §340g(4), the bank must allocate AT LEAST 10% of this net trading result — <strong>€330,000</strong> — to the "Fonds für allgemeine Bankrisiken" special reserve, shown as its OWN distinct Bilanz item, EVERY year, regardless of the bank's own overall profitability — a mandatory, quantified prudential mechanism with genuinely no direct parallel anywhere else in this poster series' documentation of ordinary corporate accounting.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>§340i — Credit-Institution-Specific Konzernabschluss Adaptations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">The same consolidation framework, with banking-specific measurement carried through</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§340i requires credit institutions to prepare a Konzernabschluss and Konzernlagebericht largely per the general §§290 et seq. framework this series has documented extensively — but with the SAME banking-specific measurement and presentation adaptations (trading book netting, maturity banding, the general banking risk reserve) carried through at the consolidated level too, ensuring the group accounts reflect the same specialised banking reality as the individual accounts.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>§340k — Prüfungsvorschriften: Where Bank and Cooperative Audit Models Meet</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely interesting overlap with the previous poster's own cooperative-audit model</div>
      <div class="principle">§340k(2) provides a distinctive rule: where the credit institution is ITSELF organised as a Genossenschaft (cooperative) or a rechtsfähiger wirtschaftlicher Verein (registered economic association), the audit is performed — DEPARTING from the ordinary §319(1) sentence 1 corporate rule established in the earlier Audit poster — by the SAME Prüfungsverband (cooperative auditing association) the institution belongs to as a member, established in the previous poster's own documentation of cooperative accounting. This is CONDITIONAL, however: more than HALF of the Prüfungsverband's own managing board members must themselves be Wirtschaftsprüfer (chartered accountants) — or, where the Prüfungsverband has only two board members, at least ONE of them must be.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Worked Example — a Cooperative Bank's Audit</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Two specialised frameworks converging on the same institution</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: A Volksbank's own audit arrangement</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A Volksbank — a credit institution organised as a registered cooperative, per the earlier Cooperatives poster — belongs to a regional Prüfungsverband whose own managing board has four members, three of whom are qualified Wirtschaftsprüfer.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Because MORE than half (three of four) of the Prüfungsverband's own board members are chartered accountants, the Volksbank's statutory audit is performed by ITS OWN Prüfungsverband under §340k(2) — genuinely combining the cooperative-specific Verbandsprüfung model established in the previous poster with the credit-institution-specific accounting framework this poster documents, rather than requiring the bank to separately engage an ordinary, freely-chosen Abschlussprüfer under §318.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>§340l — Offenlegungsvorschriften, Including Cross-Border EU/EEA Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely wider disclosure footprint than an ordinary domestic corporation faces</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§340l(1) requires credit institutions to disclose their Jahresabschluss, Lagebericht, Konzernabschluss, and Konzernlagebericht per the general §325 framework established earlier in this series. UNIQUELY, however, credit institutions that are NOT themselves branches must ALSO disclose these SAME documents in EVERY OTHER EU/EEA member state where they have established a branch — with that FOREIGN disclosure governed by the LAW of the relevant host state, not by HGB itself — a genuinely wider cross-border disclosure footprint than an ordinary domestic corporation, reflecting banks' own characteristically cross-border branch networks.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>§340m–340n — Extending Criminal Provisions to Non-Corporate-Form Institutions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The same extension logic as §335b, now for a genuinely different population</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Confirmed content extends the §§331–333 criminal provisions established many posters ago ALSO to credit institutions, financial services institutions, investment firms, and other §340-covered institutions that are NOT organised in the legal form of a Kapitalgesellschaft — directly paralleling the SAME "nulla poena sine lege certa" logic that required §335b's own separate extension to certain partnerships in the previous cluster of posters. §331 itself is further extended to cover breaches of duty by [specific further categories the source material did not fully detail] — reflecting the SAME care this series has consistently observed when extending criminal liability to genuinely new populations.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Correctly classifying trading-book versus non-trading-book positions for §340c's own netting mechanism to apply appropriately</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Navigating the separate Kreditinstituts-Rechnungslegungsverordnung (RechKredV) for the actual, detailed Formblatt structure — this poster documents the STATUTORY framework, not the regulation's own line-by-line presentation requirements</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 9)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A major divergence point — expected-credit-loss impairment versus HGB's own dual measurement approach</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">This Subsection represents one of the MOST significant IFRS divergence areas this poster series has touched: IFRS 9's own EXPECTED CREDIT LOSS impairment model for financial instruments — requiring forward-looking loss estimation from initial recognition — differs substantially from HGB's own §340e dual-measurement, incurred-loss-oriented approach, itself rooted in the SAME §253(3)/(4) fixed/current-asset distinction established very early in this series. The §340f/§340g prudential reserve mechanism, similarly, has NO direct IFRS equivalent — IFRS's own approach relies on its expected-credit-loss provisioning model rather than a separate, quantified general-risk reserve sitting alongside ordinary loss provisions.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§340–340o event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Trading book netting (§340c)</span><span><span class="badge bp">GuV</span></span><span>A single net figure, not gross lines</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Maturity banding (§340d)</span><span><span class="badge be">Anhang</span></span><span>Never shown on the Bilanz face</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>General banking risk reserve (§340g)</span><span><span class="badge bo">Bilanz</span></span><span>A separate, mandatory 10%-of-trading-income line</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Related Provisions — Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §§340–340o connect across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§330</span><span>Supplies the Formblatt-delegation power underlying the separate RechKredV regulation</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§247, §253(3)-(4)</span><span>Supplies the fixed/current-asset dual measurement logic §340e echoes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§336–339</span><span>The cooperative-specific Prüfungsverband model §340k(2) extends to qualifying cooperative banks</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§335b</span><span>The extension-to-non-corporate-forms logic §340m/n directly parallels</span></div>
      </div>
